                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_12\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix (SavedHqlExportToCSV.java) wraps the single-selection parameter handling block in `if (value != null) { ... }`, where `value = helper.getParameter(option.name())`. Before the fix, when a single-choice parameter (e.g. SUBJECT or DEPARTMENT) was not actually supplied in the request, `value` would be null, yet the code unconditionally created an `IdValue`, called `option.lookupValue(context.getUser(), value)` with a null value, and then attempted `Long.valueOf(value)` on a null string \u2014 causing a failure (NumberFormatException/NPE) that broke CSV export whenever a report contained a single-choice parameter placeholder that wasn't passed as a request parameter.\n\nTracing the history of this exact code block across the 14 candidates:\n- candidate_01 created SavedHqlExportToCSV.java originally, but at that point the file only handled parameters via a simple positional `params` string (`for (SavedHQL.Option o: ...) { ... v.setText(i < p.length ? p[i] : \"\"); ... }`). There was no `option.name()`-keyed `helper.getParameter(...)`, no `lookupValue`, and no unguarded `Long.valueOf(value)` call \u2014 i.e., the buggy pattern did not exist yet.\n- candidate_12 is the commit that introduces the entire new parameter-resolution mechanism, including the exact problematic else-branch:\n  ```\n  String value = helper.getParameter(option.name());\n  SavedHQLInterface.IdValue v = new SavedHQLInterface.IdValue();\n  v.setValue(option.name());\n  Long id = option.lookupValue(context.getUser(), value);\n  if (id == null) {\n      try { id = Long.valueOf(value); } catch (NumberFormatException e) {}\n  }\n  v.setText(id == null ? \"\" : id.toString());\n  params.add(v);\n  ```\n  This is precisely the code the fix wraps with a null check \u2014 no guard exists here for `value == null`, so this commit is where the defect was introduced.\n- candidate_13 modifies the same file extensively (adding a separate `SavedHQLParameter`/custom-parameter feature) but does not touch or alter this specific else-branch; the buggy code is carried forward unchanged.\n- The fix diff (and candidate_14, which is byte-identical to the fix diff and therefore actually represents the fix commit itself rather than a distinct candidate) finally adds the `if (value != null)` guard.\n\nSince the buggy code path was absent before candidate_12 and first appears there exactly as described by the fix, candidate_12 is the bug-introducing commit.\n"
                }
              }
